IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fa-foreshore`

-- Foreshores Act 1920, ss 4(2), 5 to 11 -- worked cases.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked
-- against a decided case or subsidiary legislation (none was retrieved).
--
-- Fixtures are generated from a table of which flags are set (scratchpad gen_foreshores-act-1920.py). Expected results were written by
-- hand from the text.

§ `s 4(2) -- who approves a reclamation`

#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 3 FALSE EQUALS `the Minister`
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 8 FALSE EQUALS `the Minister`
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 8.5 FALSE EQUALS Parliament
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 6 FALSE EQUALS `the Minister`
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 6 TRUE EQUALS Parliament
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 4 TRUE EQUALS `the Minister`
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 4.5 TRUE EQUALS Parliament
#ASSERT `the reclamation must be approved by` 200 FALSE EQUALS Parliament

§ `s 5 -- State land`

#ASSERT `earlier rights over the foreshore are extinguished` TRUE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `earlier rights over the foreshore are extinguished` TRUE FALSE

§ `ss 6, 7 -- compensation and appeal`

#ASSERT NOT `there is a right to compensation from the Government` `loss of sea frontage` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `there is a right to compensation from the Government` `physical damage to a building` TRUE

#ASSERT NOT `the person may appeal to the Minister` `loss of sea frontage` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the person may appeal to the Minister` `loss of a sea view` TRUE
#ASSERT `the person may appeal to the Minister` `physical damage to a building` TRUE
#ASSERT `the person may appeal to the Minister` `physical damage to a seawall or jetty` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `the person may appeal to the Minister` `physical damage to a building` FALSE

#ASSERT `the Minister's appeal can lead to` `payment of a sum the Minister determines`
#ASSERT `the Minister's appeal can lead to` `repairs by the appropriate authority`
#ASSERT NOT `the Minister's appeal can lead to` `damages assessed by a court`
#ASSERT NOT `the Minister's appeal can lead to` `a further appeal to the courts`

